# Distractors in Recall, Distractor-Free Recognition, and the Word Frequency Effect

**Authors:** Wallace, William P., And Others

## Abstract
Two experiments investigated word-frequency effects with modified recall and recognition testing conditions. The procedural modifications included introducing partial copy cues and including irrelevant partial copy cues (distractor cues) in recall, and eliminating distractors from recognition tests. Words with high background frequency (HF) were better recalled than low frequency words (LF), and LF were better recognized than HF. (Editor/RK)

**Source:** https://eric.ed.gov/?id=EJ188122

